# CHANGES SINCE DOOMGENERIC, UNRELEASED:

        There is no release yet. This section lists what PicoDoom adds
        to doomgeneric, from commit e257a09 of ozkl/doomgeneric. Part
        of it has not run on a device; Status says which.

##        Engine fixes:

        * -playdemo and -timedemo no longer read a dangling pointer to
          the demo lump name, which lived on the stack of D_DoomMain.

        * I_Quit exits after the exit functions have run, so the menu's
          Quit and the end of a -playdemo stop the program. A recursive
          I_Error exits instead of overflowing the stack.

        * default.cfg and doomgenericdoom.cfg are read at start and
          written at exit, from and to the working directory, so key
          bindings can be set. docs/doomgeneric.md describes the keys.

##        Wayland back end (Makefile.wayland):

        * A window in wl_shm buffers that scales the 320x200 frame to
          the size the compositor configures, fitted to 4:3 or set with
          -aspect, or filling the window with -stretch. -fullscreen,
          -appid and -title, and -keymap to bind evdev codes to keys and
          game actions. An idle inhibitor keeps the screen on while the
          window is shown, when the compositor offers the protocol.

        * Each buffer row is aligned to four bytes. An odd window width
          gave an RGB565 stride that pixman and wlroots refuse.

        * Present options, all off by default: -present-direct reads the
          engine's frame buffer without a copy, -presentmode new uses an
          unrolled RGB565 converter, -damage sbar compares the status
          bar rows with the previous frame and -damage rows compares
          every row the picture samples; only the rows that changed
          are converted and damaged, and a commit where none changed
          is skipped (-damage-rects, -damage-noskip). The settings and
          counters are printed at start and exit.

          The pictures docs/img/h2200-container-doom-title.svg,
          docs/img/h2200-container-doom-game.svg and
          docs/img/h2200-container-doom-tiled.svg show the Freedoom
          title, the game alone with the 4:3 picture centred, and the
          game in the top tile with a terminal under it, in the iPAQ
          h2200 frame. They are renders from a headless picowl in a
          container, not device captures. The tiled one shows the
          window following a size the compositor sets.

##        Renderer:

        * A direct-mapped cache of 1024 entries holds the result of
          R_GetColumn per texture column, and R_DrawColumn, R_DrawSpan
          and R_DrawFuzzColumn keep their sources and colormaps in
          locals. On an iPAQ h2200 the headless timedemo goes from 56.4
          to 67.8 frames per second. No visible difference.

##        Sound and music:

        * Sound effects through ALSA: an integer mixer for 16 voices
          and a PCM layer that uses ioctls only, so the program links
          statically on ARMv5 without libasound. -sndpcm and -sndrate
          pick the device and rate. A device that cannot be opened
          leaves the game silent with one line of output.

        * -audio-mode tick fills the device queue from the game thread
          and runs no audio thread. -audio-period, -audio-periods,
          -audio-depth and -audio-idle tune the queue and the mixing.

        * Pre-rendered music: one mono 22050 Hz FLAC file per song,
          found by a hash of the music lump in index.txt of -musicdir
          or DOOM_MUSIC_DIR, decoded with the vendored dr_flac. A song
          without a file is silent. docs/audio.md has the pack format;
          the tool that renders a pack is a plan.

##        Game data:

        * The 2019 and later Doom and Doom II IWADs, SIGIL and SIGIL II
          as -file add-ons to doom.wad, and Master Levels and No Rest
          for the Living as add-ons to doom2.wad run, with the limits in
          docs/wads.md. Full-screen patches wider than the screen are
          centred and cropped. The limits are 256 visplanes and 256
          screens of openings.

        * The main WAD and each -file WAD are inspected before loading.
          A file that is no WAD, is damaged, has no levels, lacks
          PLAYPAL or needs MBF21 or id24 DEHACKED extensions stops the
          game with a message that names the cause.

          Not shown: the screens of the re-release IWADs and of SIGIL,
          because their graphics are licensed and are not published.

##        Tests:

        * doomgeneric/test/Makefile has the targets all (mixer, tick
          fill, sound module on a fake PCM device, music), armel (the
          same under qemu-arm-static), present and present-armel (frame
          converter and damage model) and wad (WAD inspection and wide
          patches on synthetic files). docs/building.md lists what they
          need.

##        Documentation:

        * README.md, AGENTS.md, CONTRIBUTING.md, SECURITY.md and
          docs/options.md, docs/building.md, docs/audio.md,
          docs/wads.md, docs/performance.md; REUSE.toml and LICENSES/.

          The animations docs/img/startup-melt.svg and
          docs/img/demo-3s.svg show the Freedoom title, the melting
          wipe and the first level, and three seconds of the Freedoom
          demo, in the iPAQ h2200 frame. They play engine frames from a
          headless build at 35 tics a second, not device captures.

##        Status:

        * Run on an iPAQ h2200 under picowl: the Wayland back end, the
          present options, sound effects in thread and tick mode, the
          renderer changes. README.md has the table and
          docs/performance.md the measurements.

        * Not run on a device: music (the device runs had no pack), and
          everything under Game data: the re-release IWADs, SIGIL and
          SIGIL II, Master Levels and No Rest for the Living, the
          cropped wide patches and the WAD inspection. They ran
          headless and in host tests. Not measured: the h3900 and
          hx4700 with these changes.

        * No pictures: the engine fixes, sound and music, the renderer
          change (the frames are bit-identical) and the tests.

        * Known open: mouse and touchscreen input (issue 10).
